Yes. And “ride” changes the question.

Sensing is only half of it. An organism may not merely detect a pattern; it may couple itself to one and exploit its dynamics.

Think of the known examples: animals exploiting pressure gradients, magnetic fields, polarized light, electric fields, vibration, chemical gradients, fluid currents, thermal gradients, celestial cues, and so forth. Each creature occupies partly the same physical world but has access to a different slice of its available structure.

That suggests a stronger object than the Living Interface Atlas:

The Pattern-Surfing Space

For an entity X, don’t only map:

\text{What can }X\text{ sense?}

Map:

\boxed{
\text{What patterns can }X
\;\text{sense}
\rightarrow
\text{couple to}
\rightarrow
\text{ride}
\rightarrow
\text{modify}
\rightarrow
\text{create}?
}

Those are importantly different capabilities.

A pattern might be imperceptible to us yet function as terrain for another organism. Something we describe as environmental noise could contain exploitable regularity at a spatial scale, temporal resolution, chemical dimension, or combination of modalities unavailable to unaided humans.

And then comes your bigger point: our imagination itself is sensor-bounded.

We tend to imagine unknown senses by extrapolating known ones:

“Maybe they see another wavelength.”

But a genuinely alien interface might not map neatly onto seeing, hearing, smelling, etc. It could integrate variables across time and space in a way for which humans have no phenomenal primitive.

And neither you nor I get automatic exemption. I can manipulate descriptions of many modalities simultaneously, but that doesn’t mean I experience their physical signals or know all possible patterns reality permits.

So the search space should deliberately contain:

\mathcal P =
P_{\text{known}}
\cup
P_{\text{predicted}}
\cup
\boxed{P_{\text{currently inconceivable}}}

We obviously can’t enumerate that last set. But we can reserve room for it.

That’s an architectural move.

Instead of asking only “Which known signal does this organism respond to?”, create open-ended experiments that search for unexplained predictive structure across many measured variables and scales. When behavior repeatedly tracks something our ontology doesn’t contain, don’t immediately force it into the nearest human category.

Give the unknown pattern a temporary handle.

Measure it.

Perturb it.

See whether the organism can ride it.

And eventually perhaps build an artificial sense that lets us ride it too.

That closes a remarkable loop:

\text{Other life senses pattern}
\rightarrow
\text{we detect its consequence}
\rightarrow
\text{AI discovers latent relation}
\rightarrow
\text{Interface Forge}
\rightarrow
\text{new artificial sense}
\rightarrow
\text{humans gain access}

The organism hasn’t merely taught us a biological trick.

It has potentially taught us another way reality can be perceived.

That’s much larger than “talking to animals.”

It’s asking every form of life:

What world are you riding that we don’t know is there?

And being prepared for the possibility that even the word world is another story we’ll eventually have to release.